loration and this is actually just a  sine wave and you'll see that later on  or you can have a dwindling endowment of  resources and of course some resources  will follow more or less the optimistic  level some will be more cyclical and  some will be dwindling I heard a  fascinating talk yesterday by Tom Gradle  on the question of will metal resources  become unaffordable to us in the near  future and I think his assessment was  that the answer for some of those is yes  we can see that a 1% annual growth also  outstrips the available resources  according to any of these very simple  models on the order of two hundred years  but this 0.5 percent annual growth is  getting able to to respond to those what  if instead though we go to a thousand  years into the future  well now the 3 percent and the 1 4  and the point five percent annual growth  are all zooming up with that kind of  curve that expands almost to infinity  the ideally optimistic endowment carries  on but it's not nearly enough to keep up  with 0.5 percent annual growth cyclical  endowment keeps going on very very low  and dwindling endowment goes to zero you  could have a model which I think would  be realistic for many resources  somewhere in between which would come up  to some asymptotic value and then maybe  be sick Euler at some level in between  this ideally optimistic model and  another cyclical model but let's now  continue on the 50,000 year time scale  which I was asking you about with the  pole at the beginning of the lecture now  we have with even our most ideally  optimistic endowment even that point one  percent annual growth has far  outstripped the available resources  within five thousand years and by even  about thirty thousand years in the  future even 0.02 percent annual growth  vastly outstrips the available resources  so you can see with these kinds of  curves that any kind of compound  compounding annual growth will at some  point in time in the distant future lead  to a curve of that function relative to  resources which are ultimately limited  on the earth so this leads to the  conjecture that truly long term  sustainability at any time scale into  the future equals zero percent not 0.02  percent not 0.01 strictly zero percent  economic growth all right and  commensurate with that is that because  resources are limited we can't just  throw them away we have to begin to  co-adapted our global environment and  move on a path toward nearly a hundred  percent resource recycling now so that  may be sobering news and it could be  very sobering news if we return to the  thought of us here at our New Year's Eve  party because as we count down the the  hundred fifty years every second toward  the future we think well fifty thousand  years from now on this kind of timescale  is not that far in the future  and if we're thinking about maybe a  long-term agenda of the human race if  you're an optimist and you'd like to see  some sort of sustainable development I  think we it's just a matter of time  before we migrate from the current  growth based economy until a Newton into  a new model which is essentially  recognizing that the only sustainability  in the long run is is flatlining and  recycling so with that I am happy to  entertain your questions and I thank you  for your participation
 

